# Nightly copy of the media files (product images, attachments) to Cloudflare R2,
# so they survive losing Hetzner (INI-078). Install on BORO only, as root:
#     install -m 644 devops/cron/aiku-media-backup /etc/cron.d/aiku-media-backup
#
# /home/inikoo/backup_aiku.sh already copies the media to the storage box, but that
# is Hetzner too, on the same Helsinki site. On 15 Oct 2026 the media was 183 GB in
# 255,509 files (one per directory), growing about 1,100 files a day: about $2.75 a
# month in R2, and the first upload fits in the free million requests of a month.
#
# R2: a bucket of its own, aiku-media-backup, EU jurisdiction, private, and a token
# limited to that bucket with Object Read & Write and an IP filter for boro
# (65.109.156.60, 2a01:4f9:3080:1324::/64). Not the aiku-media bucket, which belongs
# to the unused media-r2 disk, and never the app's token. Then, as root:
#     rclone config create r2-media s3 provider=Cloudflare access_key_id=... secret_access_key=... \
#         endpoint=https://8c8a97c6d4117196e5275c09a53bf8ea.eu.r2.cloudflarestorage.com no_check_bucket=true
# no_check_bucket: otherwise every rclone command first tries to create the bucket,
# which this token may not do, and fails with 403 AccessDenied on CreateBucket.
#
# copy, not sync: a file deleted on boro stays in R2. It costs cents and keeps an
# image that was deleted by mistake.
# --fast-list: one listing of the whole bucket instead of one per directory, which
# would be 255,000 paid requests a night.
# --update --use-server-modtime: upload only what is new or changed since the last
# upload, read from the listing; comparing rclone's own modification time would ask
# R2 once per file.
# The first run uploads everything, start it by hand inside tmux.
#
# A failure is reported on Discord through aiku-db-backup (devops/cron/aiku-db-backup).
#
# AWS S3 too (INI-133): a second copy beside the database backup, so a server started at
# AWS reads the images with its own role, no Cloudflare key needed. Glacier Instant
# Retrieval: about $1.10 a month for 204 GB (Oct 2026), readable at once, about $6 to read
# everything back. Uses the "aws" remote of devops/cron/aiku-db-backup.
#
# Restore check, byte for byte against one top directory of the media:
#     rclone check /home/aiku/aiku/shared/storage/media r2-media:aiku-media-backup --one-way --download --include '/1G/**'
# Restore everything:
#     rclone copy r2-media:aiku-media-backup /home/aiku/aiku/shared/storage/media --transfers 32 --progress
0 4 * * * root rclone copy /home/aiku/aiku/shared/storage/media r2-media:aiku-media-backup --fast-list --update --use-server-modtime --transfers 16 --s3-no-check-bucket --log-level NOTICE --log-file /var/log/aiku-media-backup.log || /usr/local/bin/aiku-db-backup --alert "Copying the media files to R2 failed. See /var/log/aiku-media-backup.log on boro."
30 5 * * * root rclone copy /home/aiku/aiku/shared/storage/media aws:aiku-pgbackrest/aiku-media --fast-list --update --use-server-modtime --transfers 16 --s3-storage-class GLACIER_IR --log-level NOTICE --log-file /var/log/aiku-media-backup-s3.log || /usr/local/bin/aiku-db-backup --alert "Copying the media files to AWS S3 failed. See /var/log/aiku-media-backup-s3.log on boro."
